# Three counters on one page: one thing made several times.
#
# The page library has no "component", and does not need one. A kind of your
# own holds the elements that belong together, and a verb makes them. Here a
# counter is a count, which is the matter, with a note and two buttons, which
# are how it is shown. The same verb builds all three.
#
# Run it in the playground and the page appears under what it says. Run from
# the command line, it sketches the page.

Use the page library.

A counter has a title (text), a count (number), a face (note), a raiser (button) and a lowerer (button).

# A count never goes below nothing. Pressing "One fewer" at nought is refused
# by this rule, and the page is left exactly as it was.
A counter's count is never less than 0.

There is a heading called Banner whose wording is "Counters".
There is a note called Sum whose wording is "Nothing counted yet.".

# Build a counter: a panel of its own, with its note and its buttons inside.
To build a title (text):
  Let the frame be a new panel whose title is the title.
  Let the dial be a new note whose wording is "0" and whose panel is the frame.
  Let the upper be a new button whose label is "One more" and whose panel is the frame.
  Let the downer be a new button whose label is "One fewer" and whose panel is the frame.
  There is a new counter whose title is the title and whose count is 0 and whose face is the dial and whose raiser is the upper and whose lowerer is the downer.

# Show a counter's count on its own note, and the sum of them all.
To show a counter:
  Set the counter's face's wording to "{the counter's count}".
  Set Sum's wording to "{the total count of all counters} counted in all.".

Build "Apples".
Build "Pears".
Build "Plums".

# One answer serves every counter there is, or will be: it finds the counter
# that the pressed button belongs to.
Whenever a button becomes pressed:
  For each counter whose raiser is the button:
    Add 1 to the counter's count.
    Show the counter.
  For each counter whose lowerer is the button:
    Subtract 1 from the counter's count.
    Show the counter.

For example, the number of counters is 3.
For example, the number of buttons is 6.
